bb_dev: retry BB_RPC_GET_LIST instead of one 100ms shot

bb_dev_getlist() -- the RPC call every client tool (ar8030-linkctl,
ar8030-pair, ar8030-status, this project's own transport binaries) goes
through first, before it can do anything else, to find the AR8030 device
at all -- opened one fresh TCP connection to ar8030d, gave it a flat
100ms to answer BB_RPC_GET_LIST, and gave up for good on anything less
than a clean reply.

Confirmed on real hardware (S65ar8030-transport-tx/S97ar8030's own
autoreconnect loops, power-cycle testing): ar8030d can take longer than
100ms to answer this under its own entirely ordinary transient load --
several worker threads, a concurrent ar8030-pair session, a burst of
status polling -- even though the device is registered and healthy, and
the exact same query succeeds if just retried a moment later. Every
caller had no way to tell that apart from the device genuinely never
having registered, so a single missed 100ms window here surfaced
identically as "no AR8030 device known to the daemon" and drove
disproportionate reactions further up the stack: full re-pairing, or (in
this project's own boot scripts) an automatic hardware reset of the
whole chip. Retrying with a longer per-attempt timeout fixes the false
negative once, in the one function every tool already funnels through,
instead of every caller growing its own guess at a recovery timeout on
top of it.

Worst case (the device is genuinely absent) now takes under 2 seconds to
report that instead of 100ms -- a non-issue for a CLI tool, and far
cheaper than the reactions a false negative used to trigger.

